{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793308609,"nonce":"nCD6ohHZVBIBCrK2E0emv9Jl19U=","message":"Implemented and committed as 7e105d9e (Balance optimizing WASM AOT shards by body cost).\n\nThe partition weight now includes a fixed 32-instruction-equivalent body cost for optimizing WASM machine codegen (levels 1-3). Contiguous ordering and the \u003c=80-instruction inline-import threshold remain unchanged. Native targets and codegen 0 retain instruction-only weights.\n\nNUC: Intel i5-1250P, LLVM 21.1.8, matched host release O3/ThinLTO/16-CU compilers, AIR/LLVM IR O3, wasm32-wasip1-threads, fibers on, five shards. Three reversed-order measured samples per setting after warm-ups, with no concurrent compiler builds; every measured build reused all 3,552 optimized AIR bodies.\n\nCodegen 3 median backend: 8.428 -\u003e 7.662 s (-9.1%). Longest worker: 7.732 -\u003e 7.010 s. Process CPU: 55.600 -\u003e 55.315 s. Observed whole build: 32.010 -\u003e 30.106 s, but LLVM IR construction also varied, so the whole wall difference is not attributed to balancing. WASM grows 0.12%. Last-shard body count falls 5,882 -\u003e 4,675.\n\nCodegen 0's experimental body cost increased median backend 5.464 -\u003e 5.587 s (+2.2%); rejected. The final codegen-0 module is byte-identical to the baseline. The final codegen-3 module is byte-identical to the measured weighted module.\n\nChecks pass: three partition/exception/attribute unit tests, all eight optimization/catch combinations on the final NUC compiler, WASM object boundary validation, clippy, CPU/numeric and DeltaBlue checksums, and Chrome smoke on both NUC-generated final modules (233/224 frames, exit 0). Four measured kernel runs per variant show no runtime regression in these samples. Both fiber and non-fiber builds are covered.\n\nReport/raw data/reproducer: docs/wasm/shard-build-times.md, docs/wasm/shard-build-time-results.json, scripts/bench_wasm_compile.py. Raw data retains the initial experiment patch, rejected codegen-0 samples, exact compiler and module hashes, per-worker stages, load/swap counters and runtime checks. The final NUC binary is ~/ash-wasm-compile-20261029/ash-candidate; its existing dirty checkouts were preserved.\n\nRemaining tracked priorities: 3b7fa54 selective body loading; 8b12bc8 LLVM artifact reuse; 94d819f sound early elimination; 81d0887 linker/fiber overhead.","files":null}]}